--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- File:         lexiftool/TagLookup.lua
-- Ported from:  lib/Image/ExifTool/TagLookup.pm
--
-- Description:  Fast lookup for ExifTool tags
--
-- Notes:        This lookup is used to look up tag names for writing
--
-- Revisions:    2004-12-31 - P. Harvey Created
--               2013-01-06 - PH Allow wildcards in FindTagInfo()
--
-- Port notes:   Upstream's TagLookup.pm is two files in one: between
--               `#++++ Begin automatically generated code ++++` and
--               `#++++ End automatically generated code ++++` sits pure data
--               written by build_tag_lookup, everything outside it is
--               hand-written code.  This port keeps the split at exactly the
--               same place and marks it with the same two comments, so the two
--               files can be laid side by side: the code half below is a hand
--               port, the data half between the markers is produced by
--               lexiftool/BuildTagLookup.lua (run by tools/gen_tag_lookup.lua,
--               the counterpart of build_tag_lookup) from this port's own tag
--               tables and must never be hand-edited.
--
--               tableList is a Lua array, so the table numbers are 1-based,
--               and it names the port's tables ('lexiftool.Exif.Main') in the
--               order of their upstream names.  Where Perl
--               stores a reference to the root structure's tag ID as the first
--               element of a flattened tag's ID list (`[\'Regions','...']`),
--               Lua has no scalar references and the port wraps that one ID in
--               a table instead (`{{'Regions'},'...'}`) -- a value a tag ID can
--               never otherwise be, so the `ref $$ids[0]` test ports directly
--               as a type test.
--
--               The two module-body blocks that fold
--               %Image::ExifTool::UserDefined (filled by a Lua config file,
--               ExifTool.LoadConfigFiles) and @Image::ExifTool::pluginTags
--               into the lookup are ported; there are no plug-in modules yet,
--               so the second condition is always false.  AddFields' lookup
--               into %Image::ExifTool::UserDefined::xmpStruct (structure
--               names, upstream's backward compatibility only) reads an empty
--               table: a config file defines structures in place.  In Lua the two blocks have to run
--               after TagLookup.AddTags is defined rather than at their upstream
--               position, so they sit at the bottom of the file (a note marks
--               where they belong).  FindTagInfo has no wantarray: it always
--               returns the list, and a caller wanting upstream's scalar
--               context takes element 1.
--
--               The write path resolves bare tag names through this index, as
--               upstream does (`use Image::ExifTool::TagLookup qw(FindTagInfo
--               TagExists)` at the top of Writer.pl).
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

local TagLookup = {}

local ExifTool = require('lexiftool')
local perl = require('lexiftool.perl')
local isMatchDyn, subst = perl.isMatchDyn, perl.subst
local length = perl.length
local perlTruthy = perl.truthy

local remove = table.remove
local tsort = perl.tsort
local str = perl.str
local perlLc, ucfirst = perl.lc, perl.ucfirst

-- (upstream predeclares the two mutually recursive routines here --
--  sub AddTags($$); sub AddFields($$$$$$;$$); -- which Lua does not need: both
--  live on the module table, so the call resolves the field at call time)

--
-- Note: the lists below were generated by Image::ExifTool::BuildTagLookup
--

-- one lookup entry per line, exactly as upstream writes it -- a tag name that
-- occurs in many tables makes a long line, and wrapping it would break the
-- line-for-line correspondence with TagLookup.pm that the markers exist for
-- (no fixed limit: the line grows with every table the port gains, so a
-- number here would only have to be raised again and again)
-- luacheck: no max line length

--++++ Begin automatically generated code ++++

